Minimalest Possible Correct Workflow
1. Create Small Disk
dd if=/dev/zero of=mini_disk.dd bs=1M count=20
2. Format It
mkfs.ext4 mini_disk.dd
3. Create Mount Point
mkdir ~/mnt
4. Mount It
sudo mount -o loop mini_disk.dd ~/mnt
5. Add Evidence
echo "This is digital evidence" | sudo tee ~/mnt/evidence.txt
6. Unmount
sudo umount ~/mnt
7. Open in Autopsy
sudo autopsy

Then open:

http://127.0.0.1:9999/autopsy

Add:

mini_disk.dd

Experiment complete.

When dc3dd actually matters

dc3dd is intended for:

imaging real disks (/dev/sda, /dev/sdb)
preserving evidence
generating hashes
maintaining forensic integrity

In your reduced lab setup:

you already directly created the image
so re-imaging the image is redundant

You can still mention in viva/report:

“dc3dd acquisition step was omitted because the created .dd file itself already represented the forensic disk image.”
